### When do you accept data loss?

When the cost of guaranteeing durability exceeds the cost of losing the data.

| Data Class | Cost of Loss | RPO | Durability Strategy |
|---|---|---|---|
| Financial transactions | Catastrophic | 0 (zero) | Sync replication, acknowledged commits |
| Ownership records | Catastrophic | 0 | Same as above |
| Telemetry/metrics | Near zero | 60 seconds | Buffered, async flush |
| Derived analytics | Recomputation cost | Minutes | Recompute from source of truth |
| Cache entries | Cache-miss cost | Total loss OK | Regenerable on miss |

### Why is mixing data classes on one path dangerous?

```java
// WRONG: telemetry and transfers share the same strong-durability pipeline
// Telemetry volume is 100x transfers
// Telemetry flood saturates the replication link → transfers delayed!

// CORRECT: separate paths matching each class's RPO
transferRepo.save(transfer);         // sync replication, acknowledged
telemetryBuffer.append(metric);       // async flush, 60s buffer
cache.put(key, value);                // in-memory, total loss OK
```

### How do you define the loss window?

For each data class, state a Recovery Point Objective: the maximum data (measured in time) you are willing to lose.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class DurabilityPolicy {
    // Financial: zero-loss → sync replication, acknowledged commit
    public static final Duration TRANSFER_RPO = Duration.ZERO;

    // Telemetry: 60-second window → async buffer, flush every 60s
    public static final Duration TELEMETRY_RPO = Duration.ofSeconds(60);

    // Cache: total loss acceptable → in-memory only
    public static final Duration CACHE_RPO = Duration.ofDays(999); // effectively infinite
}
```

### Why must the mechanism match the stated window?

```java
// Claiming 60-second telemetry RPO but actually buffering 5 minutes:
// A crash loses 5 minutes, not 60 seconds → the stated RPO is a lie
// Size the buffer to the RPO: bufferSize = throughput × RPO
```

### How do you verify the RPO?

```java
@Test
void transferLossWindowIsZero() {
    // Simulate crash immediately after acknowledged commit
    BigDecimal before = totalMoney();
    transferService.execute("SAV2001", "SAV3002", new BigDecimal("50000"));
    simulateCrash();
    restartAndRecover();
    assertEquals(before, totalMoney()); // no money lost
}

@Test
void telemetryLossWithin60Seconds() {
    telemetryService.record(metric);
    simulateCrashBeforeFlush();
    restartAndRecover();
    // At most 60 seconds of telemetry lost → acceptable
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: treating all data equally
// Forces cheap telemetry through expensive strong-durability path
// Saturates the critical pipeline → endangers financial transactions!

// CORRECT: classify, define RPO, route to matching path
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The loss window must be stated explicitly, not left vague.
- Each RPO drives concrete engineering choices: replication mode, flush frequency, buffer size.
- In a regulated bank, these trade-offs must be auditable.
- Verify by crashing in the exact window between acknowledgement and durable commit.

### What are the edge cases?

The trickiest edge case is data that looks low-value until it is needed for a regulatory audit. Telemetry that includes transaction correlation IDs might be needed months later for fraud investigation. Define the retention requirement with compliance before classifying data as losable.

```java
package com.bank.data;

public class DataClassificationEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Edge case 1: telemetry with correlation IDs");
        System.out.println("  Looks low-value, but needed for fraud investigation");
        System.out.println("  Fix: keep correlation-bearing telemetry durable, discard raw metrics");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Edge case 2: cache that holds the only copy");
        System.out.println("  If the source DB is down, cache is the ONLY data");
        System.out.println("  Fix: never treat cache as 'total loss OK' if it is the last copy");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Edge case 3: derived data whose source is expensive to recompute");
        System.out.println("  Recomputing 30 days of analytics takes 6 hours");
        System.out.println("  Fix: RPO should reflect recomputation cost, not just data value");
    }
}
```

